# Cloudflare Pages — routing rules for the Axal VC frontend.
#
# Pages evaluates these top-to-bottom and stops at the FIRST match, which is
# why the /assets/* rule has to come first. It replicates the behaviour of the
# Worker's "hardened static-asset serving" (cloudflare-worker/src/index.ts,
# Task #37): a hashed asset that no longer exists must 404 as itself, never
# fall back to index.html. Without this rule the catch-all below would answer
# a stale /assets/index.abc123.js with HTML and a 200, the browser would try
# to execute HTML as a JS module, and the page renders blank — the recurring
# Safari blank page after a deploy. A real 404 is what lets the boot watchdog
# in index.html and the stale-chunk recovery in main.jsx reload onto the
# current build.
#
# Difference from the Worker, stated so it is not mistaken for a regression:
# the Worker answers with a plain-text body, this answers with the branded
# 404.html. Both carry a 404 status, which is the part that matters.
/assets/*    /404.html      404

# Everything else that is not a real file in this build — client-side routes
# like /deals/:id — falls through to the SPA so the router can handle it.
# The 31 prerendered routes in docs/ are real files and are served directly,
# never reaching this rule.
/*           /index.html    200
